--TEST--
`$uri->compareTo` is a total order and not only an equality test: antisymmetric, reflexive and transitive over a corpus, decided component by component in the readers' declared order rather than over the text, with an absent component sorting before an empty one — spec § 12, `rule:classes/comparable`, `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::compareTo
// The neighbouring case asserts *what* § 6.2.2 normalizes before the
// comparison happens, row by row. This one asserts the thing no row can show:
// that the answer is an order at all. `rule:classes/comparable` makes `compareTo` the member
// `Comparable` declares, so a member that merely answered `0` for equal and
// something plausible otherwise would pass every row above and still be unfit
// for a sort.
//
// The corpus is ten references differing in one component each, plus one that
// differs from the first only in the two things § 6.2.2 folds — so a zero
// appears off the diagonal as well as on it.
array<string> $corpus = [
    "http://a.example/p",
    "http://b.example/a",
    "https://a.example/p",
    "http://a.example/p?q",
    "http://a.example/p#f",
    "http://a.example:81/p",
    "http://u@a.example/p",
    "HTTP://A.EXAMPLE/p",
    "mailto:x@a.example",
    "/relative",
];

// All 100 ordered pairs, counted rather than read off a line: the answer is
// always one of the three literals `rule:classes/comparable` names, it reverses when the
// operands do, and it is `0` exactly on the diagonal plus the one pair the
// normalization makes equivalent. A member ordering by text would still
// satisfy the first two and fail the last.
int $pairs = 0;
int $antisymmetric = 0;
int $inThree = 0;
int $reflexive = 0;
int $zeros = 0;
foreach ($corpus as string $left) {
    foreach ($corpus as string $right) {
        $pairs = $pairs + 1;
        var $ab = Core\Uri::parse($left)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($right));
        var $ba = Core\Uri::parse($right)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($left));
        if ($ab == 0 - $ba) { $antisymmetric = $antisymmetric + 1; }
        if (($ab == 0 - 1) || ($ab == 0) || ($ab == 1)) { $inThree = $inThree + 1; }
        if ($ab == 0) { $zeros = $zeros + 1; }
        if ($left == $right) {
            if ($ab == 0) { $reflexive = $reflexive + 1; }
        }
    }
}
echo "pairs ", $pairs, " antisymmetric ", $antisymmetric, " in {-1,0,1} ", $inThree, "\n";
echo "reflexive ", $reflexive, " zeros ", $zeros, "\n";

// Transitivity is the property a sort actually depends on, and the only one
// that needs three references to state. All 1000 ordered triples: wherever
// `$a` does not follow `$b` and `$b` does not follow `$c`, `$a` does not
// follow `$c`.
int $triples = 0;
int $transitive = 0;
foreach ($corpus as string $a) {
    foreach ($corpus as string $b) {
        foreach ($corpus as string $c) {
            $triples = $triples + 1;
            var $x = Core\Uri::parse($a)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($b));
            var $y = Core\Uri::parse($b)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($c));
            var $z = Core\Uri::parse($a)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($c));
            if (($x < 1) && ($y < 1)) {
                if ($z < 1) { $transitive = $transitive + 1; }
            } else {
                $transitive = $transitive + 1;
            }
        }
    }
}
echo "triples ", $triples, " transitive ", $transitive, "\n";

// Which order it is, named on its own lines: the components in the order the
// readers are declared, earlier deciding before later. `http` sorts before
// `https` even though its host sorts after, and the host decides before the
// path for the same reason.
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://z.example/z")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("https://a.example/a")), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example/z")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://b.example/a")), "\n";

// The port is a number, not the text of one, which is the cheapest witness
// that the comparison runs over components at all: `:9` sorts before `:10`
// while the two references' own text sorts the other way.
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example:9/z")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example:10/a")), "\n";
echo Core\Str::compare("http://a.example:9/z", "http://a.example:10/a"), "\n";

// The bound every nullable reader brings with it: an *absent* component sorts
// before an *empty* one, and before any present value. A relative reference
// has no scheme, `…/` has no query, and `…/?` has one that is the empty
// string — three distinct references, not two.
echo Core\Uri::parse("/relative")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example/")), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example/")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example/?")), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example/")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example/#")), "\n";

// `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`: `==` on two objects is identity and there is no `__equals`, so
// two `Uri`s that compare equal are still two objects. `compareTo($o) == 0` is
// the spelling for "the same URI", and this is the pair that tells the two
// questions apart.
var $one = Core\Uri::parse("http://a.example/p");
var $two = Core\Uri::parse("HTTP://A.EXAMPLE/p");
echo $one->compareTo($two), "\n";
if ($one == $two) { echo "same object\n"; } else { echo "two objects\n"; }
if ($one == $one) { echo "identity\n"; }
--EXPECT--
pairs 100 antisymmetric 100 in {-1,0,1} 100
reflexive 10 zeros 12
triples 1000 transitive 1000
-1
-1
-1
1
-1
-1
-1
0
two objects
identity
